l ok so let's  talk about binge eating disorder so the  dsm-5 defines  eating disorder that's consuming in a  specific amount of time a specific  amount of food that is definitely larger  than what somebody would otherwise  consume in similar circumstances in a  similar time period now I know a lot of  you guys are probably gonna try to  defend your favorite milk bangers that's  having a really fast metabolism or  working out really hard or maybe even  clean most of time so that they can  afford their cheat meal but unless you  Michael Phelps you probably don't need  10,000 calories in one sitting and even  if you did you don't need to consume all  said 10,000 calories in pizza wings  fries and other kind of highly processed  foods that are really stark departure  from your usual diet like by all means  have wings and pizza and fries but like  you can pepper them into your normal  usual diet you don't have to eat them  all in one go it's actually quite  possible that this restrict binge  repente repeat cycle is like part of  these youtubers daily dietary regime but  they're able to maintain their body  weight and size as a result of punishing  restriction over exercising and other  dietary measures that are put in place  to help counteract these large amounts  of food so I just want to reiterate that  even if these youtubers don't gain  weight as a result of these occasional  kind of look bangs or eating stunts it  doesn't mean it's okay or healthy non  the long-term short-term or from mental  or physical health I also really hate  the term cheat day because it assigns  this moral value to food that the dieter  has decided is bad when had they just  enjoyed these foods in mindful moderate  portions regularly there wouldn't have  been this need to go overboard with the  moral transgressions you can cheat on  your spouse and you can like cheat on  your hairdresser but you can't cheat on  food so we really need to shift the  conversation and stop this disorderly  talk 